<title>Little Joys Next Five Creators</title>
<meta name="claude:viewport-fit-sides" content="cover">
<link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Bricolage+Grotesque:opsz,wght@12..96,600;12..96,700&family=Source+Sans+3:ital,wght@0,400;0,600;0,700;1,400&family=IBM+Plex+Mono:wght@400;500&display=swap">
<style>
/* Layout: one reading column; the answer (five cards) first, then evidence that can be opened as needed. */
:root {
  --bg: #f6f7f3; --surface: #ffffff; --fg: #1c2229; --muted: #5a6470; --rule: #dde2da;
  --accent: #2c6a58; --accent-soft: #e2efe9; --avoid: #9a5a12; --avoid-soft: #f6ead9;
  --neutral-soft: #eceee9; --mark: #f1d77a;
  --display: "Bricolage Grotesque", "Segoe UI", system-ui, sans-serif;
  --body: "Source Sans 3", "Segoe UI", system-ui, sans-serif;
  --mono: "IBM Plex Mono", ui-monospace, Consolas, monospace;
}
@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) { :root:not([data-theme="light"]) {
  --bg: #121614; --surface: #1a1f1c; --fg: #e5e9e3; --muted: #9aa49d; --rule: #2c3430;
  --accent: #7cc3aa; --accent-soft: #1f3a31; --avoid: #e2a85c; --avoid-soft: #3a2c19;
  --neutral-soft: #242a26; --mark: #6b5a1f; color-scheme: dark } }
:root[data-theme="dark"] {
  --bg: #121614; --surface: #1a1f1c; --fg: #e5e9e3; --muted: #9aa49d; --rule: #2c3430;
  --accent: #7cc3aa; --accent-soft: #1f3a31; --avoid: #e2a85c; --avoid-soft: #3a2c19;
  --neutral-soft: #242a26; --mark: #6b5a1f; color-scheme: dark }

body { background: var(--bg); color: var(--fg); font: 400 17px/1.55 var(--body); }
.wrap { max-width: 1040px; margin: 0 auto; padding-block: 40px 72px;
  padding-left: max(16px, env(safe-area-inset-left, 0px)); padding-right: max(16px, env(safe-area-inset-right, 0px));
  display: grid; gap: 56px; }
h1, h2, h3 { font-family: var(--display); line-height: 1.15; text-wrap: balance; margin: 0; }
h1 { font-size: clamp(2rem, 4.5vw, 3rem); letter-spacing: -0.01em; }
h2 { font-size: 1.6rem; }
h3 { font-size: 1.15rem; }
p { margin: 0; max-width: 68ch; }
a { color: var(--accent); }
a:focus-visible, summary:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--accent); outline-offset: 2px; }
.eyebrow { font: 600 0.78rem/1 var(--body); letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--muted); }
.lede { font-size: 1.2rem; color: var(--fg); }
.muted { color: var(--muted); }
.mono, code { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.88em; }
.num { font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
section { display: grid; gap: 20px; }
.head { display: grid; gap: 14px; }
.source { font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--muted); }
.source code { background: var(--neutral-soft); padding: 1px 5px; border-radius: 3px; }

/* answer cards */
.cards { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(185px, 1fr)); gap: 14px; }
.card { background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 10px; padding: 18px;
  display: grid; gap: 10px; align-content: start; min-width: 0; }
.card .rank { font: 700 0.85rem var(--mono); color: var(--muted); }
.card h3 { font-size: 1.1rem; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
.card .product { font-weight: 600; font-size: 0.95rem; }
.card .hook { font-size: 0.93rem; color: var(--muted); }
.bars { display: grid; gap: 5px; font-size: 0.78rem; color: var(--muted); }
.bar { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 46px 1fr 30px; align-items: center; gap: 8px; }
.bar i { display: block; height: 6px; border-radius: 3px; background: var(--neutral-soft); position: relative; overflow: hidden; }
.bar i b { position: absolute; inset: 0 auto 0 0; background: var(--accent); border-radius: 3px; }
.chip { display: inline-block; font: 600 0.74rem/1 var(--body); letter-spacing: 0.03em; padding: 5px 8px;
  border-radius: 999px; background: var(--neutral-soft); color: var(--fg); width: fit-content; }
.chip.robust, .chip.strong { background: var(--accent-soft); color: var(--accent); }
.chip.suggestive { background: transparent; color: var(--accent); box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px var(--accent); }
.chip.thin, .chip.costlier { background: var(--avoid-soft); color: var(--avoid); }
.chip.cheaper { background: var(--accent-soft); color: var(--accent); }
.chip.average { background: var(--neutral-soft); color: var(--muted); }
.chip.risk { background: var(--avoid-soft); color: var(--avoid); }
.riskbox { background: var(--avoid-soft); border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; display: grid; gap: 6px; }
.riskbox .eyebrow { color: var(--avoid); }
.caveat { display: block; font-size: 0.8rem; color: var(--avoid); }
.claims li { display: grid; gap: 4px; }
.chip.marginal, .chip.driven, .chip.chosen { background: var(--avoid-soft); color: var(--avoid); }
.chip.out, .chip.inconclusive, .chip.not, .chip.excluded { background: var(--neutral-soft); color: var(--muted); }

.callout { background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 10px; padding: 18px 20px;
  display: grid; gap: 8px; }
.callout.key { border-left: 4px solid var(--accent); }

/* tables */
.tablewrap { overflow-x: auto; border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 10px; background: var(--surface); }
table { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; font-size: 0.93rem; }
th, td { text-align: left; padding: 10px 12px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); vertical-align: top; }
th { font: 600 0.78rem var(--body); letter-spacing: 0.04em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--muted); white-space: nowrap; }
tr:last-child td { border-bottom: 0; }
td.num, th.num { text-align: right; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; white-space: nowrap; }
tr.picked td { background: var(--accent-soft); }

/* chart */
.forest { width: 100%; height: auto; min-width: 560px; display: block; }
.forest .tick { font: 500 11px var(--body); fill: var(--muted); }
.forest .rowlab { font: 500 13px var(--body); fill: var(--fg); }
.forest .grid { stroke: var(--rule); stroke-width: 1; }
.forest .axis0 { stroke: var(--muted); stroke-width: 1.5; stroke-dasharray: 3 3; }
.forest .ci { stroke-width: 3; stroke-linecap: round; }
.forest .ci.do { stroke: var(--accent); } .forest .ci.avoid { stroke: var(--avoid); }
.forest .pt.do { fill: var(--accent); } .forest .pt.avoid { fill: var(--avoid); }
.forest .faint { opacity: 0.4; }
.chartbox { background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 12px; overflow-x: auto; }
.legend { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 16px; font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--muted); }
.legend span::before { content: ""; display: inline-block; width: 10px; height: 10px; border-radius: 50%; margin-right: 6px; vertical-align: -1px; }
.legend .l-do::before { background: var(--accent); } .legend .l-avoid::before { background: var(--avoid); }
.legend .l-faint::before { background: var(--muted); opacity: 0.4; }

/* creator drill-down */
details.brief { background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 10px; }
details.brief > summary { cursor: pointer; padding: 16px 20px; display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 8px 16px;
  align-items: baseline; list-style: none; }
details.brief > summary::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }
details.brief > summary::before { content: "▸"; color: var(--muted); }
details.brief[open] > summary::before { content: "▾"; }
details.brief > summary h3 { font-size: 1.2rem; }
.brief-body { padding: 4px 20px 22px; display: grid; gap: 22px; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); padding-top: 18px; }
.brief-body .two { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr)); gap: 22px; }
.brief-body .two > div { min-width: 0; display: grid; gap: 8px; align-content: start; }
.brief-body ul { margin: 0; padding-left: 1.1em; display: grid; gap: 6px; }
.hookq { font-size: 1.05rem; background: var(--accent-soft); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px; }
.script td:first-child { font-family: var(--mono); white-space: nowrap; color: var(--muted); }
.links { font-size: 0.88rem; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }

ol.method { margin: 0; padding-left: 1.2em; display: grid; gap: 12px; max-width: 72ch; }
ul.plain { margin: 0; padding-left: 1.1em; display: grid; gap: 8px; max-width: 72ch; }
.weights { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(210px, 1fr)); gap: 12px; }
.weights div { background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px; font-size: 0.9rem; min-width: 0; }
.weights dl { margin: 6px 0 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr auto; gap: 4px 10px; }
.weights dd { margin: 0; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
footer { font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--muted); border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); padding-top: 16px; }
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) { details.brief > summary::before { transition: transform .15s; } }
</style>

<div class="wrap">

<header class="head">
  <span class="eyebrow">Little Joys · creator selection · data to 26 Oct 2026</span>
  <h1>Which five creators next, and what should they say?</h1>
  <p class="lede">Work with these five creators, and have each one film a real child tasting Proteinmix or Nutrimix on camera,
    in English or Hinglish. In Little Joys' own ads, videos with a child tasting on camera got purchases about
    {{ ingredients[0].effect }} than comparable ads of the same product (95% range: {{ ingredients[0].range }}).
    That is a consistent pattern across our checks, not proof: the ad account is small.</p>
  <p class="muted">Built from Little Joys' own data only: {{ facts.n_ads }} ads, {{ facts.n_creators }} creators and
    their {{ facts.n_posts }} recent posts. Ad spend, purchases and revenue in this exercise are made-up numbers, treated here as if they were the real ad account.</p>
</header>

<section id="answer" aria-labelledby="answer-h">
  <h2 id="answer-h">The five</h2>
  <div class="cards">
  {% for b in briefs %}
    <article class="card">
      <span class="rank">#{{ loop.index }}</span>
      <h3>@{{ b.handle }}</h3>
      <span class="product">{{ b.product }}</span>
      {% if b.chosen_over_model %}<span class="chip chosen">Analyst's pick over the model's #5</span>
      {% else %}<span class="chip {{ b.robustness }}">{{ 'Holds under almost any weighting' if b.robustness == 'robust' else 'Holds under most weightings' }}</span>{% endif %}
      {% if b.risks %}<span class="chip risk">Check audience before booking</span>{% endif %}
      <div class="bars" aria-label="Scores out of 1">
        {% for k in ['fit', 'reach', 'safety'] %}
        <div class="bar"><span>{{ k|capitalize }}</span><i><b style="width: {{ (b[k] * 100)|round(0) }}%"></b></i><span class="num">{{ '%.2f'|format(b[k]) }}</span></div>
        {% endfor %}
      </div>
      <p class="hook">{{ b.why[0] }}</p>
      <a href="#brief-{{ loop.index }}">Read the brief</a>
    </article>
  {% endfor %}
  </div>
  <p class="source">Scores: <code>outputs/phase2_reasons.csv</code> from <code>src/phase2_creators.py</code>. Briefs: <code>outputs/phase3_briefs.json</code> from <code>src/phase3_briefs.py</code>.</p>
</section>

<section id="what-works" aria-labelledby="works-h">
  <div class="head">
    <h2 id="works-h">What works in Little Joys' own ads</h2>
    <p>We compared ads with and without each ingredient on <strong>cost per purchase</strong> (spend ÷ purchases, all ads pooled).
      Every comparison is adjusted for the product advertised, because products differ a lot: Proteinmix
      {{ facts.product_cpa.proteinmix }} per purchase, Nutrimix {{ facts.product_cpa.nutrimix }}, gummies
      {{ facts.product_cpa.gummies }}, everything else {{ facts.product_cpa.other }}. It is also adjusted for when the ad was
      switched off. The bars are 95% ranges: where a bar crosses "same", the data cannot tell the difference from zero.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="chartbox">
    {{ forest|safe }}
  </div>
  <div class="legend"><span class="l-do">Go with cheaper purchases</span><span class="l-avoid">Go with more expensive purchases</span><span class="l-faint">Not reliable (rests on one ad, or too few ads)</span></div>

  <div class="tablewrap">
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Ingredient</th><th>Evidence</th><th class="num">Ads with it</th><th class="num">Cost per purchase<br>with / without</th><th>Adjusted effect (95% range)</th><th>Without {{ facts.top_ad }}</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
    {% for r in ingredients %}
      <tr>
        <td>{{ r.name }}{% if r.small_side %}<span class="caveat">Small comparison group: {{ r.small_side }}</span>{% endif %}</td>
        <td><span class="chip {{ r.verdict_key }}">{{ r.verdict }}</span></td>
        <td class="num">{{ r.n }}</td>
        <td class="num">{{ r.cpa_with }} / {{ r.cpa_without }}</td>
        <td>{{ r.effect }} <span class="muted">({{ r.range }})</span></td>
        <td>{{ r.without_top }}</td>
        <td class="mono">{{ r.examples }}</td>
      </tr>
    {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>
  <div class="callout key">
    <h3>Why nothing here says "proven"</h3>
    <p>With {{ facts.n_ads_used }} ads, about {{ facts.n_tests }} comparisons were tested. After allowing for that many tries, the
      best result still falls short of our bar for "strong" (q = {{ facts.best_q }}, bar 0.10). "Suggestive" means the effect held
      after adjusting for product and stop date, held again when the launch date was used instead of the stop date, and
      still held with the single best ad removed. One ad, {{ facts.top_ad }} ({{ facts.top_ad_cpa }} per purchase), brought in
      {{ facts.top_ad_share }} of all purchases. Without it, the child-tasting effect is {{ ingredients[0].without_top }}.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="callout">
    <h3>How much the label check matters for the headline</h3>
    <p>The AI tagged {{ taste[0].n }} ads as "child reacts to the taste". A person checked every one of them against the transcript
      and video frames: {{ taste_check.yes }} confirmed, {{ taste_check.no }} wrong (removed), {{ taste_check.unclear }} unclear
      (a child drinks or eats, but no reaction is visible in the frames). The result barely moves:</p>
    <div class="tablewrap"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Which ads count</th><th class="num">Ads</th><th>Adjusted effect (95% range)</th><th>Without {{ facts.top_ad }}</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{% for t in taste %}<tr><td>{{ t.version }}</td><td class="num">{{ t.n }}</td><td>{{ t.effect }} <span class="muted">({{ t.range }})</span></td><td>{{ t.without_top }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}</tbody>
    </table></div>
  </div>
  <p class="source">Source: <code>outputs/phase1_winning_ingredients.csv</code>, <code>outputs/phase1_label_effects.csv</code> from <code>src/phase1_ads.py</code>.</p>
</section>

<section id="briefs" aria-labelledby="briefs-h">
  <div class="head">
    <h2 id="briefs-h">Briefs</h2>
    <p>Each brief builds the winning ingredients into something the creator already films. Claims are limited to ones Little Joys
      has already run in its ads.</p>
  </div>
  {% for b in briefs %}
  <details class="brief" id="brief-{{ loop.index }}" {% if loop.first %}open{% endif %}>
    <summary><h3>#{{ loop.index }} {{ b.name }} <span class="muted">@{{ b.handle }}</span></h3><span>{{ b.product }}</span></summary>
    <div class="brief-body">
      {% if b.risks %}<div class="riskbox"><span class="eyebrow">Risk to check before booking</span>{% for r in b.risks %}<p>{{ r }}</p>{% endfor %}</div>{% endif %}
      <div class="two">
        <div><span class="eyebrow">Why them</span><ul>{% for w in b.why %}<li>{{ w }}</li>{% endfor %}</ul></div>
        <div><span class="eyebrow">Suggested hook</span><p class="hookq">{{ b.hook }}</p>
             <span class="eyebrow">Format</span><p>{{ b.format }}</p></div>
      </div>
      <div><span class="eyebrow">Script outline</span>
        <div class="tablewrap"><table class="script"><tbody>{% for t, beat in b.script %}<tr><td>{{ t }}</td><td>{{ beat }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}</tbody></table></div>
      </div>
      <div class="two">
        <div><span class="eyebrow">Claims to make, strongest first</span><ul class="claims">{% for c in b.claims %}<li><span>{{ c.text }}</span>
          <span class="chip {{ c.strength }}">{{ c.strength_words }}</span>
          <span class="muted">{{ c.n_ads }} ad{{ 's' if c.n_ads > 1 }}, {{ c.cpa }} per purchase together; e.g. {{ c.examples }}</span></li>{% endfor %}</ul></div>
        <div><span class="eyebrow">Avoid</span><ul>{% for a in b.avoid %}<li>{{ a }}</li>{% endfor %}</ul></div>
      </div>
      <div><span class="eyebrow">Expected performance</span><ul>{% for e in b.expected %}<li>{{ e }}</li>{% endfor %}</ul></div>
      <p class="links"><span class="eyebrow">Evidence</span><br>
        Ads (open the video): {% for a in b.ad_refs %}<a class="mono" href="{{ video[a.ad_id] }}">{{ a.ad_id }}</a> ({{ a.cpa }}){{ ', ' if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}<br>
        Creator posts: {% for u in b.post_refs %}<a href="{{ u }}">{{ u|replace('https://www.', '') }}</a>{{ ', ' if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}</p>
    </div>
  </details>
  {% endfor %}
  <p class="source">Source: <code>outputs/phase3_briefs.json</code>, <code>outputs/phase3_claims_library.csv</code> from <code>src/phase3_briefs.py</code>.</p>
</section>

<section id="others" aria-labelledby="others-h">
  <h2 id="others-h">Why not the other {{ others|length }}</h2>
  <div class="tablewrap">
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th class="num">Model rank</th><th>Creator</th><th>Status</th><th>Main reason</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
    {% for o in others %}
      <tr><td class="num">{{ '—' if o.robustness == 'excluded' else o.rank }}</td><td>@{{ o.handle }}</td>
        <td><span class="chip {{ o.robustness }}">{{ {'excluded': 'Excluded', 'out': 'Not picked', 'marginal': 'Close call', 'robust': 'Close call'}[o.robustness] }}</span></td>
        <td>{{ o.one_line_reason }}</td></tr>
    {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>
  <p class="muted">Excluded means a paid post for a direct kids-nutrition competitor in the last {{ recent_days }} days (decision taken at review).</p>
  <p class="source">Source: <code>outputs/phase2_reasons.csv</code>; competitor brands classified in <code>annotations/brand_categories.csv</code>.</p>
</section>

<section id="how" aria-labelledby="how-h">
  <h2 id="how-h">How we know this</h2>
  <ol class="method">
    <li><strong>Checked the data.</strong> {{ facts.n_ads }} ads, {{ facts.spend_lakh }} spend, {{ facts.purchases }} purchases. Left out
      {% for k, v in facts.excluded %}{{ k }} ({{ v }}){% endfor %}. {{ facts.n_video_dupes }} videos had each run twice and {{ facts.n_script_dupes }} more scripts were near-copies,
      so the {{ facts.n_ads_used }} ads count as {{ facts.n_clusters }} independent creatives.</li>
    <li><strong>Read the product from the ad itself.</strong> The AI's product label filed {{ facts.pm_mislabelled }} of the
      {{ facts.pm_n }} Proteinmix ads under "other", next to sauces and spreads. Proteinmix is the cheapest product per purchase, so
      the product is taken from each ad's own text instead. This roughly halved some earlier effect sizes; every number here uses the corrected product.</li>
    <li><strong>Compared ad ingredients</strong> on cost per purchase. Each comparison was adjusted for product and for the date the
      ad was switched off, and resampled {{ facts.n_boot }} times to get the ranges, keeping re-used videos together so they never count twice.
      Every finding was then re-checked three ways: with the excluded ad put back, with the single best ad removed, and adjusting for
      launch date instead of stop date (ads are often stopped because they did badly, so the stop date partly reflects the result).</li>
    <li><strong>Checked the AI labels by hand</strong> against transcripts and video frames, including every ad tagged with the headline
      label: {{ agree_all.agree }} of {{ agree_all.checked }} checks agreed ({{ agree_all.rate|pct }}, 95% range {{ agree_all.lo95|pct }}–{{ agree_all.hi95|pct }}).
      Wrong labels on the headline ingredient were corrected before the analysis.</li>
    <li><strong>Scored creators</strong> on fit with those ingredients, reach and safety, using the weights below, then re-ranked
      them {{ facts.n_sens }} times with the weights shifted at random.</li>
    <li><strong>Wrote briefs</strong> using only claims Little Joys has already run, each linked to the ads and posts it rests on. Each
      claim is rated against ads for the same product, so a cheap product does not make its claims look good by itself.</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="tablewrap">
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Label we relied on</th><th class="num">Agreed / checked</th><th class="num">Rate (95% range)</th><th>Where it went wrong</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
    {% for a in agree if a.label != 'ALL' %}
      <tr><td class="mono">{{ a.label }}</td><td class="num">{{ a.agree }} / {{ a.checked }}</td>
        <td class="num">{{ a.rate|pct }} ({{ a.lo95|pct }}–{{ a.hi95|pct }})</td><td class="muted">{{ a.disagreements if a.disagreements == a.disagreements else '' }}</td></tr>
    {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>

  <h3>Scoring weights</h3>
  <div class="weights">
    {% for group, ws in weights.items() %}
    <div><strong>{{ group }}</strong><dl>{% for k, v in ws.items() %}<dt>{{ k|replace('_', ' ') }}</dt><dd>{{ '%.2f'|format(v) }}</dd>{% endfor %}</dl></div>
    {% endfor %}
  </div>

  <h3>Does the top five survive other weightings?</h3>
  <div class="tablewrap">
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Creator</th><th class="num">In top 5 when weights are shifted</th>{% for c in scen_cols %}<th class="num">{{ scen_labels[c] }}</th>{% endfor %}</tr></thead>
    <tbody>
    {% for r in ranking if r.handle in picked or (r.rank_base <= 8 and not r.excluded_competitor_deal) %}
      <tr class="{{ 'picked' if r.handle in picked }}"><td>@{{ r.handle }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.top5_share_local|pct }}</td>
        {% for c in scen_cols %}<td class="num">#{{ r[c] }}</td>{% endfor %}</tr>
    {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>
  <p class="source">Source: <code>outputs/phase2_ranking.csv</code>, <code>outputs/phase1_label_agreement.csv</code>, <code>annotations/label_spotcheck.csv</code>.</p>
</section>

<section id="limits" aria-labelledby="limits-h">
  <h2 id="limits-h">Confidence and limitations</h2>
  <ul class="plain">
    <li><strong>Small sample.</strong> {{ facts.n_ads_used }} ads. No ingredient reaches "strong"; the top findings are "suggestive".
      One ad ({{ facts.top_ad }}) brought in {{ facts.top_ad_share }} of all purchases and dominates the Proteinmix numbers.</li>
    <li><strong>No ad in this data features any of the five</strong>, so each creator's results are inferred from ads of the same product
      built the same way. Proteinmix lookalikes: {{ comps.proteinmix.n }} ads, {{ comps.proteinmix.all_cpa }} per purchase together
      (range {{ comps.proteinmix.all_lo90 }}–{{ comps.proteinmix.all_hi90 }}), {{ comps.proteinmix.no_LJ004_cpa }} without {{ facts.top_ad }}.
      Nutrimix lookalikes: {{ comps.nutrimix.n }} ads, {{ comps.nutrimix.all_cpa }} (range {{ comps.nutrimix.all_lo90 }}–{{ comps.nutrimix.all_hi90 }})
      against {{ comps.nutrimix.product_cpa }} for all Nutrimix ads. Treat these as a direction, not a forecast.
      @storiesbyvihaa has worked with Little Joys before; that ad cannot be identified in this export, so ask the brand team for its results.</li>
    <li><strong>Language.</strong> No ad ran in Telugu or Gujarati. Ads in other regional languages cost {{ facts.regional_pct }} more per
      purchase after adjustment, and the 10 pure-Hindi ads also did worse (not conclusive), so every brief is in English or Hinglish,
      with a Telugu cut of the #1 video as an optional side test.</li>
    <li><strong>Audience fit is mostly unknown.</strong> Only {{ facts.n_audience }} of {{ facts.n_creators }} creators have audience data. For
      @storiesbyvihaa it shows a mostly male audience, which is flagged on her card as a check before booking.</li>
    <li><strong>Performance numbers are synthetic</strong> for this exercise; the patterns are only as real as the export they come from.</li>
    <li><strong>AI labels are mostly right</strong> ({{ agree_all.rate|pct }} on our checks), with two exceptions that were fixed: the product
      label (Proteinmix filed as "other") and the creator-post language label (it describes the caption, not the spoken language, so
      transcripts were used instead).</li>
    <li><strong>Judgement calls</strong> are written down and editable: which brands count as competitors, the scoring weights,
      and the choice of ohmother.in over the model's #5 (the two are close to a tie under shifted weights).</li>
